  "notes": "ETYMOLOGY:\n{歓|かん} (joy, pleasure) + {喜|き} (rejoice, delight). Both characters express happiness, creating an emphatic compound.\n\nUSAGE:\nA literary and elevated word for joy. {歓喜|かんき}する means \"to rejoice greatly.\" Often used in news reporting for sports victories, election results, and similar dramatic moments. Beethoven's \"Ode to Joy\" is known in Japanese as {歓喜|かんき}の{歌|うた}.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {歓喜|かんき}の{声|こえ}: shouts of joy\n- {歓喜|かんき}の{涙|なみだ}: tears of joy\n- {歓喜|かんき}に{包|つつ}まれる: to be enveloped in joy\n- {歓喜|かんき}に{沸|わ}く: to erupt in joy\n- {歓喜|かんき}する: to rejoice\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {喜|よろこ}び: joy, happiness (everyday, general)\n- {歓声|かんせい}: cheers, shouts of joy (the sound itself)",
